{{was wotd|2024|March|4}}

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|la|latet anguis in herbā|lit=a snake hides in the grass}}, from ''[[w:Eclogues|Eclogue]]'' III by the Roman poet {{w|Virgil}} (traditionally 70&nbsp;– 19 {{B.C.E.}}).<ref>{{cite-book|author=Virgil|authorlink=Virgil|chapter=[[w:Eclogues|[Eclogue] III]]|translator=[[w:Henry Rushton Fairclough|H[enry] Rushton Fairclough]]|title=Virgil{{nb...|with an English Translation by H. Rushton Fairclough, Professor of Latin in Stanford University, California: In Two Volumes}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Heinemann (publisher)|William Heinemann]]; New York, N.Y.: [[w:G. P. Putnam|G[eorge] P[almer] Putnam’s Sons]]|year=1930|volume=I (Eclogues, Georgics, Aeneid I–VI)|lines=92–93|pages=26–27|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/virgil.-works.-vol-1.-loeb/Virgil.Works.Vol1.Loeb/page/26/mode/1up|oclc=9774799|passage=Qui legitis flores et humi nascentia fraga, / frigidus, o pueri, fugite hine, latet anguis in herba|translation=Ye who cull flowers and low-growing strawberries, away from here, lads; a chill snake lurks in the grass.}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|subentry=a snake in the grass|subid=1617637729|entry=snake|pos=n|id=4943491308|date=July 2023}}</ref><ref>{{R:Collins English Dictionary|pos=n}}</ref> Compare {{noncog|nl|addertje onder het gras}}. 

